# Veripsa release gate (auto-discovered). One gate per file -> two PRs adding gates add two FILES, never a
# shared line -> no false merge conflict on registration. register_gate is defined in run_gates.sh.
# Format: register_gate <test> <marker> <name> <ok_blurb> <desc> [tail]   (no dollar/backtick in the text)
register_gate "tests/test_sec_definer_revoke.py" "SEC DEFINER REVOKE GATE: PASS" "sec definer revoke" "three SECURITY DEFINER helpers that trusted a caller-set identity (core.current_account GUC / a p_account arg) now strip the PUBLIC EXECUTE default and (for the tombstone fn) re-derive identity from the connection role -- a buyer/seat is DENIED calling them directly, cannot forge a cross-tenant conclusion tombstone (silent-miss) or read another tenant's policy, while the legit App path still writes the order-independence tombstone in its own routed tenant" "Locks a verified SECURITY finding: Postgres grants EXECUTE to PUBLIC by default on every CREATE FUNCTION, and three SECURITY DEFINER fns were missing the REVOKE EXECUTE FROM PUBLIC that every sibling gate fn carries (30_gate.sql alone has 21). (1) core._conclude_change_tombstone arms a claim write trusting p_account/the current_account GUC -- a customer DB seat could SET core.current_account to a victim tenant and forge a change:__concluded__ tombstone there, making core.change_concluded() return true so the webhook SKIPS analysis = a CROSS-TENANT SILENT MISS. (2)/(3) core._policy_int and core._policy_text read core.policy by the caller-set current_account without re-pinning = a cross-tenant policy read. Reproduced before the fix (forged victim tombstone landed; victim policy value 999 / a secret glob leaked). The fix: REVOKE EXECUTE FROM PUBLIC on all three, plus defense-in-depth re-derive of the writing account/agent in the tombstone fn via establish_session_write_context (the legit land_change/release_change callers already pass the same derived values, so the owner path is byte-identical). This gate stands up the ephemeral Postgres + a 2-tenant fixture (DEMO != ACME, a real victim policy row seeded through the gate) and proves live: a buyer/seat role (PUBLIC + veripsa_writer) is DENIED (permission denied for function) on all three fns and has_function_privilege is false for every buyer class while the owner has it; the forged cross-tenant tombstone does NOT land in the victim; the buyer cannot read the victim's policy values; and the legit App path on a routed installation still writes the tombstone in its OWN resolved tenant with change_concluded() reporting true, including when the App forges current_account to a different tenant (the write follows the connection-role identity). Content-free (ids/paths/counts only)." 30
